.SS "TLS interception on a forward listener"
.PP
A CONNECT tunnel is opaque by design: the proxy sees a name and a byte count and nothing else, so a destination policy is the only policy it can apply. \fBintercept\fR changes that. The proxy answers the client\(aqs handshake with a certificate it signs itself, opens its own TLS connection to the destination, and relays the plaintext between the two \[u2014] which is what lets YARA rules, and everything else that reads bytes, see inside HTTPS.
.PP
This is the one feature here that makes a proxy \fIless\fR safe if it is built carelessly, because it replaces a connection the client verified end to end with two connections the client cannot see past. Three things follow from that, and none of them is optional.
.PP
.TS
