.PP
\fBThe destination is verified first, and only then is a certificate forged.\fR A client never sees a forged certificate for a server whose own certificate did not verify \[u2014] it sees the handshake fail, which is what it would have seen with no proxy in the way. An interception proxy that gets this backwards turns every verified connection through it into an unverified one while leaving the padlock in place, which is worse than not intercepting at all. \fBverify_upstream: false\fR exists as a key so that turning it off is a decision somebody wrote down.
.PP
\fBThe signing key can impersonate every site to every client that trusts the CA.\fR It is refused if its mode allows anyone but its owner to read it, both by \fBxproxy check\fR and at startup.
.PP
\fBSome traffic must not be read at all\fR, whatever the estate\(aqs policy says: banking, health, anything carrying somebody\(aqs own credentials. \fBbypass_hosts\fR is where that is written, and it is consulted before anything is decrypted \[u2014] a rule that another rule can overtake is not that rule.
.PP
Two more things the proxy refuses rather than guesses. A tunnel whose first bytes are not a TLS ClientHello is spliced through untouched: CONNECT carries SSH, database protocols and anything else, and answering a handshake to something that was not offering one breaks it for no reason (\fBforward_intercept_passed\fR). And a handshake whose server name disagrees with the host in the CONNECT is refused (\fBforward_sni_mismatch\fR), because a tunnel opened to one name and a handshake for another is somebody reaching a destination the policy checked against a different one. A tunnel opened to an \fIaddress\fR is the exception and not a hole: there the policy checked the address, the bytes reach that address whatever the handshake says, and the name only picks a virtual host once they arrive.
.PP
The issued certificate carries the \fIreal\fR certificate\(aqs names \[u2014] its SANs, its IP addresses, its common name \[u2014] so a client that pins a name still works and one that pins a key still fails, as it should. The cache is keyed on the name and on the real certificate\(aqs fingerprint, so a destination that rotates its certificate gets a fresh forgery rather than a stale one. \fBalpn\fR defaults to \fBhttp/1.1\fR alone: a stream the proxy relays is one it has to be able to read, and offering \fBh2\fR without parsing HTTP/2 is how an interception proxy breaks a site.
.PP
\fBsocks5\fR tunnels on the same listener are intercepted by the same rules: a destination reachable in either protocol on one port under one policy is not a policy if one of the two walks past it.
.PP
An intercepted connection writes a \fBforward_intercept\fR access line with the client, user, destination, negotiated ALPN and the TLS version reached upstream. Counters: \fBforward_intercepted\fR, \fBforward_intercept_refused\fR, \fBforward_intercept_passed\fR and \fBforward_intercept_bytes\fR.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
\- name: egress
  address: "0.0.0.0:3128"
  kind: forward
  forward:
    ports: [80, 443]
    auth: {users_file: /etc/xproxy/proxy.htpasswd}
    intercept:
      ca_cert_file: /etc/xproxy/mitm\-ca.pem
      ca_key_file: /etc/xproxy/mitm\-ca\-key.pem
      hosts: ["*.example.com", "*.cdn.test"]
      bypass_hosts: ["*.bank.test", "*.health.test"]
      yara: {rules_dir: /etc/xproxy/yara}
.ft
.fi
.RE
.SS "MASQUE on a forward listener"
.PP
HTTP \fBCONNECT\fR tunnels TCP, and that is all it tunnels. Everything else an estate sends \[u2014] DNS, QUIC, NTP, WireGuard, telemetry \[u2014] either leaves the network outside this policy or does not leave at all, and the first is the usual answer. CONNECT\-UDP (RFC 9298) is the same explicit proxy for datagrams, with the same destination rules, credentials, logs and bans; CONNECT\-IP (RFC 9484) carries IP packets, which is how a MASQUE VPN is built.
.PP
.TS
