\fBGET /v1/dns\fR and \fBxproxyctl dns\fR show per listener counters (queries, cache hits and entries, blocked, refused, dropped, SERVFAIL, truncated, upstream failures); \fBDELETE /v1/dns\fR and \fBxproxyctl dns purge\fR empty the caches. Metrics: \fBxproxy_dns_*{listener}\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].smtp (kind: smtp)"
.PP
A \fBkind: smtp\fR listener is a protocol\-aware SMTP proxy. It speaks one session to the client and a second one to the upstream, and decides for itself where every command and every message ends, writing each one out again rather than passing bytes through. That is the whole point of it: a layer 4 splice carries the same octets, but then the client and the mail server each parse them on their own, and every SMTP smuggling bug there has ever been lives in the gap between two such parses.
.PP
What follows from that:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A line must end with CRLF. A bare LF is refused (\fBbare_newlines: reject\fR) or repaired to CRLF (\fBconvert\fR); a bare CR inside a line is always refused. Either way the two ends see the same line structure.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
The end of a message is \fBCRLF.CRLF\fR and nothing else, decided once, on the stream the proxy itself writes. Dot stuffing is passed through untouched, so only the terminator is interpreted.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBCHUNKING\fR and \fBBDAT\fR are never advertised or relayed: BDAT carries a length instead of a terminator, so relaying it would put the decision back in two places.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A reply the proxy cannot parse is never passed on. The client gets \fB421\fR and the session ends, because a reply the proxy did not understand is exactly the one the client would read differently.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Anything pipelined behind \fBSTARTTLS\fR ends the session with \fB554\fR (\fBstarttls_injection\fR, a \fBsmtp_denied\fR ban reason). Those octets were written before the client could see the \fB220\fR, so they were meant to be plaintext for one side and ciphertext for the other \[u2014] CVE\-2011\-0411. After the handshake the session forgets its greeting and its authentication, as RFC 3207 section 4.2 requires, and the upstream leg is reset with it.
.RE
.PP
.PP
The listener takes \fBaddress\fR, \fBsmtp\fR, and \fBtls\fR when a TLS mode needs a certificate; \fBproxy_protocol\fR works as on an HTTP listener. Bans and the global connection limits apply at accept. Changing the \fBsmtp\fR section rebinds the listener on reload.
.PP
.TS
