.PP
\fBThe secure upgrade\fR is \fBtls_mode: none\fR with \fBupstream_tls_mode: implicit\fR: a device that can only send clear syslog over UDP writes to this listener, and the records leave it as RFC 5425 TLS. It does not make the sender trustworthy \[u2014] between the device and this port the records are still in clear and still forgeable \[u2014] so put the port where only those devices can reach it, keep \fBallow_senders\fR tight, and use \fBhostname: observed\fR.
.PP
A message the relay cannot parse is refused, not forwarded: its facility, severity and host are exactly the fields every rule here decides on, and a record nobody could read is a record nobody can filter. A message with no timestamp is given the time the relay saw it, because a record nobody can order is a record that is hard to use.
.PP
On a delimited stream a message over the bound is dropped and the connection carries on \[u2014] the reader skips to the next line ending, so one long line does not cost every record behind it. On a counted stream it ends the connection, because refusing to read the octets a frame declared leaves the reader at an offset nobody knows.
.PP
Counters: \fBsyslog_received\fR, \fBsyslog_forwarded\fR, \fBsyslog_dropped\fR, \fBsyslog_refused\fR, \fBsyslog_rate_limited\fR, \fBsyslog_redacted\fR, \fBsyslog_queue_dropped\fR, \fBsyslog_send_failed\fR, \fBsyslog_connections\fR, \fBsyslog_rejected\fR. Refusals are \fBsyslog_denied\fR for the ban triggers.
.SS "server.listeners[].vnc (kind: vnc)"
.PP
A \fBkind: vnc\fR listener is a VNC gateway: the proxy is an RFB server to the client and an RFB client to the target, terminating the handshake of RFC 6143 on both legs.
.PP
\fBWhy this is not a \fBtcp\fR listener.\fR RFB\(aqs whole policy surface is in the handshake. Which security type is used, whether the session is encrypted and how, what the desktop is called \[u2014] all of it is settled in the first few hundred bytes. A proxy that does not sit in that negotiation cannot decide any of it, and cannot record what follows.
.PP
Terminating both legs is also what separates the two credentials. What a person proves to the gateway is not the desktop\(aqs password: the gateway opens the target\(aqs leg with its own, so the shared VNC password of a machine never has to be given to the people who use it.
.SS "What is supported"
.PP
\fBProtocol versions.\fR 3.3, 3.7 and 3.8, on either leg and independently: a 3.3 client can reach a 3.8 server through here. A client announcing a version nobody defines (Apple\(aqs 3.889, or anything above 3.8) is treated as the highest defined version at or below it. Below 3.3 is refused.
.PP
The versions differ in one place that matters to a gateway: before 3.8, a successful \fBnone\fR carries no \fBSecurityResult\fR at all (RFC 6143 \[u00A7]7.1.3). Each leg follows the rule of the version settled on that leg, so a 3.3 viewer is not sent four bytes it would read as the start of the desktop\(aqs dimensions, and a 3.7 target is not waited on for a message it will never send.
.PP
\fBSecurity types.\fR Only the ones with a published specification are mediated \[u2014] that is, completed on both legs, which is what makes the session recordable:
.PP
.TS
