.PP
\fBtls\fR is warned about because it is anonymous Diffie\-Hellman with no certificate to check: it stops a reader and not an active attacker. \fBvencrypt\fR with an X.509 subtype is the one to use.
.PP
\fBVeNCrypt subtypes\fR: \fBx509\-none\fR, \fBx509\-vnc\fR, \fBx509\-plain\fR, \fBtls\-none\fR, \fBtls\-vnc\fR, \fBtls\-plain\fR. The \fBtls\-*\fR ones are warned about for the same reason. The bare \fBplain\fR subtype (no TLS at all) is refused: it would send the credential in clear.
.PP
\fBReimplemented, which is not the same as supported.\fR \fBmslogon2\fR is UltraVNC\(aqs own type. There is no specification for it; what is implemented here follows the shape of UltraVNC\(aqs \fBvncauth.cpp\fR and \fBDH.cpp\fR and the public reimplementations that agree with them, and \fBinteroperability against a real UltraVNC server is not verified by this project\(aqs tests\fR. Enabling it produces a warning at load that says so.
.PP
What it is worth is worth stating plainly, because the name sounds like security: MS\-Logon II is Diffie\-Hellman over \fB64 bits\fR with the shared secret used as a \fBDES\fR key. Both have been breakable for decades, and anyone who records the exchange can recover the Windows credential inside it. It is here because the desktops exist \[u2014] reaching them through a gateway that records the session, asks for a factor and holds the policy is better than reaching them directly \[u2014] and not because the type protects anything. Put a TLS\-wrapped or SSH\-tunnelled leg around it (\fBtls_mode: wrap\fR, or \fBssh\fR) if the credential matters, which it does.
.PP
Two things follow from it carrying a \fBname\fR as well as a password, which most of RFB does not:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
It can carry a second factor without a certificate, which is the one practical advantage it has over VeNCrypt\(aqs plain subtype: \fBmfa\fR is allowed with \fBmslogon2\fR in \fBsecurity_types\fR.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Towards a target it needs both halves of a credential, so \fBupstream_user\fR and \fBupstream_password_file\fR are required when \fBupstream_security: mslogon2\fR \[u2014] refused at load rather than at the first session.
.RE
.PP
.PP
\fBTightVNC\(aqs type 16\fR is the easiest of these to be confident about, because it is not a cipher at all. It is a negotiation: the server offers a list of \fItunnels\fR and a list of \fIauthentications\fR, each named by a capability record, and the two ends pick one of each. What actually protects anything is whichever authentication it settles on.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
This gateway \fBoffers no tunnels\fR, and refuses a target that offers only tunnels. A tunnel is another protocol wrapped around this one, which would be a session the gateway can neither read nor record.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
It offers \fBNOAUTH__\fR and, where \fBpassword_file\fR is set, \fBVNCAUTH_\fR \[u2014] the same DES challenge as the \fBvncauth\fR type, checked against the gateway\(aqs own password. An authentication outside the list it offered is refused, as with any other type.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Tight sends one more block \fBafter \fBServerInit\fR\fR, listing the message types and encodings the server has beyond the standard ones. A target\(aqs is read and dropped, and a Tight client is told there are none. That is deliberate rather than a gap: what is advertised there is TightVNC\(aqs extensions, including \fBfile transfer\fR, and a gateway that cannot see inside them has no business passing them through.
.RE
.PP
.PP
\fBApple Remote Desktop\(aqs type 30\fR is Diffie\-Hellman over a prime the server chooses, MD5 of the shared secret as an AES\-128 key, and a fixed credential blob encrypted under it in ECB. Apple\(aqs own servers offer a \fB512\-bit\fR prime, the key derivation is MD5 and the mode is ECB, so the credential is protected against very little; the warning at load says so. In the server role this gateway generates a 1024\-bit prime instead \[u2014] the length is on the wire and a client reads it, but a client that assumes 512 would not interoperate. Its credential carries a name, so \fBmfa\fR works with it and \fBupstream_user\fR is required to use it towards a target.
.PP
\fBRealVNC\(aqs RSA\-AES\fR, types 129, 130 and 133, is reimplemented on the same terms and with a different balance of risk. Each end sends an RSA public key, each seals a random under the other\(aqs key, the session keys are hashed out of the two randoms, and everything after that travels in AES\-EAX boxes with a counter for a nonce.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
The \fBcryptography is not guessed at\fR: RSA and the hashes are Go\(aqs standard library, and EAX is implemented in \fBinternal/eax\fR against the published vectors of the EAX paper and NIST SP 800\-38B, which the tests run. What is reconstructed is the \fBorder and framing of the messages\fR, from TigerVNC\(aqs implementation and its \fBrfbproto\fR. Getting that wrong shows up as a handshake that does not complete and a log line naming the step \[u2014] not as a session that looks encrypted and is not.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBrsa\-aes\fR is AES\-128 with SHA\-1, \fBrsa\-aes\-256\fR is AES\-256 with SHA\-256, and \fB\fBrsa\-aes\-ne\fR protects the handshake only: the session after it is in clear.\fR That is the one thing about this family easiest to get wrong, so validation warns about it and the access log names the security type of both legs.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe listener needs an RSA key of its own\fR (\fBrsa_key_file\fR, PEM, PKCS#1 or PKCS#8, at least 2048 bits, not readable by anyone else), because each end is identified by a key.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBTowards a target the key is pinned\fR (\fBupstream_rsa_fingerprint\fR), and validation requires it. Nothing else authenticates the far end of that exchange, and unlike a viewer there is nobody at a proxy to show a fingerprint to and ask. The fingerprint is this project\(aqs own spelling \[u2014] the SHA\-256 of the key as the protocol encodes it \[u2014] and the gateway logs the one a target offered, which is where the setting is copied from.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Its credential carries a name, so \fBmfa\fR works with it and \fBupstream_user\fR is required to use it towards a target.
.RE
.PP
.PP
\fBWhat is not supported, and why.\fR These are the vendors\(aq own, with no published specification to write against and none reimplemented here:
.PP
.TS
