.SS "VNC over TLS, and VNC over SSH"
.PP
Three ways to stop the session crossing a network in clear. The two that encrypt the client\(aqs leg are alternatives; the third is the target\(aqs leg and composes with either.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBVeNCrypt\fR, negotiated inside RFB (\fBtls_mode: negotiated\fR, the default). The socket carries RFB from the first byte, and the certificate in the listener\(aqs \fBtls\fR section is presented inside the handshake. This is the one a modern viewer offers by itself, and the \fBx509\-*\fR subtypes are the ones with a certificate to check. The security type \fBtls\fR (18) works the same way, with no certificate checked.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA socket that is TLS from the first byte\fR (\fBtls_mode: wrap\fR). The client connects with TLS and speaks RFB inside it, which is what a viewer reaching a \fBstunnel\fR\-wrapped port does.
.PP
\fBwrap\fR together with the \fBvencrypt\fR or \fBtls\fR security type is a configuration error rather than two layers: the first byte a client sends is either a TLS record or \fBRFB 003.008\fR, so a port is one or the other. An estate with both kinds of viewer uses one listener for each, which is what the viewers are already pointed at.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fB\fBssh\fR\fR, for the target\(aqs leg. The gateway opens an SSH connection and reaches the VNC server through it, so the RFB never crosses the network in clear even when the server itself speaks only RFB. This is the usual \fBssh \-L\fR arrangement, done once by the gateway rather than by every operator.
.RE
.PP
.PP
.TS
