.PP
The default is none, so \fBchannels.allow: [rdpdr]\fR on its own gives a session the channel and no redirection on it. The policy is applied to the \fBdevice announcement\fR: nothing can be redirected that was not announced, so filtering that one message decides the whole of it without the gateway having to understand the traffic that follows. A refused device is taken out of the announcement, counted, written to the security log and marked in the recording. Refusing all of them leaves a valid announcement of no devices rather than a broken channel.
.PP
A device announcement the gateway cannot read \[u2014] a compressed one \[u2014] ends the session rather than passing through, because a redirection policy that quietly did not apply is worse than a session that stops.
