.SS "The two credentials"
.PP
With \fBupstream_user\fR, \fBupstream_domain\fR and \fBupstream_password_file\fR, the desktop is opened with the gateway\(aqs own account and the person\(aqs credential stops at the gateway. Without them, what the person typed is forwarded as it arrived, which is what an estate that wants its own accounts audited on the desktop needs. Either way the gateway sees the credential, which is the price of being able to check anything about it \[u2014] and the reason the listener should be reachable only over a network you trust.
.SS "The second factor"
.PP
\fBmfa\fR checks a one\-time code \fBbefore the credential reaches the desktop\fR. RDP has nowhere to ask a question \[u2014] there is no prompt in the protocol and the client is waiting for a desktop rather than a dialogue \[u2014] so the code travels with the password, after a comma:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
Password: hunter2,492013
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same arrangement the FTP relay uses, and one that works with every client because it asks nothing of the client. The code is taken off before the password goes anywhere, so the desktop never sees it.
.PP
One honest difference from the other gateways here: the factor is checked before the \fBcredential\fR reaches the desktop, not before the desktop is dialled. RDP\(aqs connection sequence requires the desktop to answer before the client sends its credential, so the TCP connection is already open by then. What the desktop never receives without a verified factor is the credential.
.SS "The recording"
.PP
The file holds the desktop\-to\-client stream \[u2014] what was on the screen \[u2014] with the timing of it, in the asciicast v2 container the other recordings use, named \fB*.rdp.cast\fR. Its header carries \fBXPROXY_PROTOCOL: rdp\fR and the security protocol of the desktop\(aqs leg, the first mark names the session and the channels it was granted, and every refused device is marked where it happened.
.PP
\fBIt is not a video.\fR The event data is the protocol stream, so replaying it needs a player that speaks RDP rather than a terminal. That is the deliberate choice: decoding at capture time would mean implementing every graphics encoding a desktop might choose \[u2014] and silently losing whatever was not implemented \[u2014] while a decoder written against this file later loses nothing.
.PP
Counters: \fBrdp_sessions\fR, \fBrdp_sessions_open\fR, \fBrdp_rejected\fR, \fBrdp_refused\fR, \fBrdp_recorded\fR, \fBrdp_mfa_ok\fR, \fBrdp_mfa_failed\fR, \fBrdp_channels_refused\fR, \fBrdp_devices_refused\fR, \fBrdp_legacy_sessions\fR (desktop legs opened with the protocol\(aqs own encryption). Every session writes one \fBrdp\fR access line with both security protocols, the routing token, the user and domain, the channels asked for and granted, and how it ended. Refusals are \fBrdp_denied\fR deny events, so bans apply.
.SS "server.listeners[].telnet (kind: telnet)"
.PP
A \fBkind: telnet\fR listener is a telnet gateway: the proxy is a telnet server to the client and a telnet client to the target, reading the NVT protocol of RFC 854 in both directions.
.PP
\fBTelnet carries everything in clear.\fR The session, every password typed into the target\(aqs own login, and the one\-time code if this listener asks for one all cross the network as plain bytes. Wrapping the listener in TLS (a \fBtls\fR section, which is what \fBtelnets\fR on 992 is) is the only thing that changes that, and validation warns every time it is left off. This listener exists because the equipment that speaks only telnet exists, not because telnet is acceptable.
.PP
\fBWhy this is not a \fBtcp\fR listener.\fR Telnet\(aqs options are commands escaped into the byte stream: \fBIAC\fR (255) begins one, \fBIAC IAC\fR is a literal 255, and everything else is data. A proxy that does not parse that cannot tell a window\-size negotiation from the characters a person typed \[u2014] which it has to, to record the session as it was seen, to decide which options a client may turn on, and to write a prompt of its own into the stream before the target is dialled.
.PP
.TS
