.PP
\fBOptions.\fR The default list is what an interactive session needs and nothing else: \fBecho\fR, \fBsuppress\-go\-ahead\fR, \fBbinary\fR, \fBterminal\-type\fR, \fBnaws\fR, \fBterminal\-speed\fR, \fBend\-of\-record\fR, \fBtiming\-mark\fR, \fBstatus\fR.
.PP
The others this proxy can name, none of them on by default:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.5i) lbx
lw(1.5i) lx.
T{
Option
T}	T{
Why it is not default
T}
T{
\fBenviron\fR,\: \fBnew\-\:environ\fR
T}	T{
Carry variables of the client\(aqs choosing to the target,\: which is how a login shell is given a different \fBPATH\fR. Validation warns when either is allowed
T}
T{
\fBx\-\:display\fR
T}	T{
Names an X display the target will try to reach,\: which is a connection back out of the estate
T}
T{
\fBauthentication\fR
T}	T{
Negotiated differently by every implementation that has it;\: the proxy would relay it without understanding it
T}
T{
\fBencryption\fR
T}	T{
Would encrypt the session end to end \[u2014] a session this proxy can no longer record or hold to a policy
T}
T{
\fBlinemode\fR,\: \fBflow\-\:control\fR
T}	T{
Accepted if you name them;\: left out because the default is character\-at\-a\-time,\: which is what a recording wants
T}
.TE
.PP
An option this proxy has no name for is always refused, whatever the lists say: one whose effect it cannot name is one it cannot hold to a policy. A refusal is answered to the side that asked (\fBWILL\fR and \fBWONT\fR are declined with \fBDONT\fR, \fBDO\fR and \fBDONT\fR with \fBWONT\fR) rather than dropped, because a refusal the asker never hears is a negotiation that repeats forever. Each one writes a \fBtelnet_option_refused\fR line and a mark in the recording, so an operator asked why a terminal behaves oddly can see that the proxy is why.
.PP
\fBThe second factor.\fR Telnet has no authentication for a proxy to read, so \fBmfa\fR is a prompt the proxy writes into the stream and an answer it reads back, before the target is dialled at all \[u2014] a client that cannot answer never reaches the equipment. It asks for a login name, then for a code, with the code not echoed. The name is only what the enrolment is looked up by: the target\(aqs own login happens afterwards and is untouched, and whether the two names agree is the target\(aqs business rather than this proxy\(aqs. A name with no enrolment is refused where \fBrequire_enrolment\fR is on.
.PP
While the proxy is asking its own questions it agrees to no options: anything the client negotiates then is declined, since the only thing at the far end so far is the proxy.
.PP
\fBThe recording\fR is the asciicast v2 format the ssh bastion writes, so the same player replays it. \fBnaws\fR gives it the window size, and a resize during the session is recorded as one. \fBinput: true\fR records what was typed as well as what was shown, and on telnet that means every password typed into the target\(aqs own login \[u2014] which the proxy does not otherwise see. The warning that applies to the bastion applies here more strongly.
.PP
Counters: \fBtelnet_sessions\fR, \fBtelnet_sessions_open\fR, \fBtelnet_rejected\fR, \fBtelnet_refused\fR, \fBtelnet_options_refused\fR, \fBtelnet_recorded\fR, \fBtelnet_mfa_ok\fR, \fBtelnet_mfa_failed\fR. Every session writes one \fBtelnet\fR access line with the client, the name the factor was checked against, the target, how it ended and how many options were refused. Refusals are \fBtelnet_denied\fR deny events, so bans apply.
.SS "server.listeners[].ftp (kind: ftp)"
.PP
A \fBkind: ftp\fR listener is a protocol\-aware FTP proxy: the proxy is an FTP server to the client and an FTP client to the target, and it is one end of every data connection as well.
.PP
\fBThe data connection is why this cannot be a \fBtcp\fR listener.\fR Every transfer in FTP happens on a second connection whose address one side announces to the other, in the body of a reply. A proxy that forwards that reply has told the client to go round it: the file then travels between the client and the target with nothing in the middle, and the control connection it did read is a list of instructions for a transfer it never saw. So the address is replaced with the proxy\(aqs own, and the proxy listens on one side and dials the other.
.PP
Only the \fIport\fR the target announced is used. The proxy dials the host its control connection is already talking to, so a target that answers with an address of its choosing cannot send the proxy somewhere else.
.PP
\fBFTP is old enough to have an attack named after it.\fR \fBPORT\fR and \fBEPRT\fR ask the server to connect back to an address the client names, and a server that obeys is a port scanner and a relay for anyone who can log in \[u2014] the bounce attack of CERT CA\-1997\-27. Active mode is off by default. With \fBallow_active: true\fR the announced address must be the client\(aqs own and the port must not be privileged; that check is the whole of the defence, which is why it is stated here rather than assumed.
.PP
.TS
