.SS "server.listeners[].ssh.principals"
.PP
Without this list a bastion has one policy for everyone in \fBauthorized_keys\fR: the deployment robot may run what the on\-call engineer may run. Each entry names who it covers and what they may do, and the listener\(aqs own settings are what an entry leaves unset \[u2014] so an entry that only moves someone to another account on the target says only that.
.PP
The list is the policy. Once there is one entry, a key no entry covers is refused at authentication rather than served under the listener\(aqs default, because falling back would be the opposite of what the list says.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.3i) lbw(0.7i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.3i) lw(0.7i) lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBname\fR
T}	T{
name
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
What the access log and the security events call this principal,\: so a refusal names a person rather than a fingerprint
T}
T{
\fBfingerprints\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
SHA256 fingerprints of the keys this entry covers,\: in the form \fBssh\-\:keygen \-\:lf\fR prints: \fBSHA256:\fR and the base64 of the digest
T}
T{
\fBcert_\:principals\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Certificate principals this entry covers. Needs \fBtrusted_\:user_\:ca_\:keys\fR: a certificate is matched by the names its CA signed into it,\: and by the key it carries
T}
T{
\fBusers\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR (any)
T}	T{
Login names the entry applies to,\: so one key can be one thing as \fBdeploy\fR and another as \fBroot\fR
T}
T{
\fBpolicy\fR
T}	T{
object
T}	T{
inherit
T}	T{
What this principal may do;\: see below
T}
.TE
.PP
An entry that names neither a fingerprint nor a certificate principal matches every key, which is how a list ends in a default. It must be the last entry, because an entry after it could never be reached.
.PP
The policy object takes \fBupstream_user\fR, \fBallow_channels\fR, \fBallow_requests\fR, \fBallow_subsystems\fR, \fBallow_commands\fR, \fBallow_env\fR, \fBforward\fR, \fBremote_forward\fR and \fBsftp\fR, each meaning what it means on the listener and each falling back to the listener when unset, plus:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.6i) lbw(0.6i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(0.6i) lw(0.6i) lw(0.8i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBdeny\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Refuse this principal outright. It is how a key stays in \fBauthorized_\:keys\fR while the person it belongs to is on leave,\: without the list losing the record that they exist
T}
.TE
.PP
A denied principal never reaches the target: the refusal is at authentication, before a channel or an upstream connection exists.
.PP
A principal\(aqs \fBrecording\fR replaces the listener\(aqs, which is how one entry is recorded and another is not; \fBrecording: {enabled: false}\fR is how a principal is spared where the listener records.
.PP
An entry that brings its own \fBsftp\fR section to a listener that has none also inherits the default that section implies: file transfer helpers are refused for that principal, because \fBscp\fR beside a careful \fBsftp\fR policy is the policy with a door next to it.
.SS "server.listeners[].ssh.recording"
.PP
The access log says a session happened. It cannot say what was done in it, because what was done is a stream of control sequences inside the channel. This writes that stream to a file per channel, in the asciicast v2 format, so "what did they actually run" is a question with an answer that is watched rather than reconstructed:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
asciinema play /var/log/xproxy/sessions/session\-20260921\-143022.100\-alice.cast
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
The format is line oriented, so a recording cut short by a crash or by \fBmax_file_bytes\fR still plays up to where it stops, and it is text, so the usual tools work on it. It is a stream, not a transcript: what the person saw is what a terminal makes of it, which means reading one is replaying it.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbw(0.6i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lw(0.6i) lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBenabled\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Present so a principal can turn a listener\(aqs recording off;\: there is no reason to write it as \fBtrue\fR
T}
T{
\fBdirectory\fR
T}	T{
path
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
Where the files go. It must exist: the proxy does not create it,\: because where these files live is a decision to make rather than to inherit
T}
T{
\fBfile_\:prefix\fR
T}	T{
name
T}	T{
\fBsession\fR
T}	T{
Begins each file name,\: which is then the time and the login,\: and always ends \fB.\:cast\fR
T}
T{
\fBinput\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Record what was typed as well as what was shown. It warns,\: and the warning is the point: a terminal\(aqs input stream carries what the screen never showed,\: which includes every password typed into a \fBsudo\fR or \fBsu\fR prompt
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:file_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB33554432\fR
T}	T{
Bounds one recording,\: counted in session bytes;\: 4096..4294967296. Past it the session carries on and the file says it stopped
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:files\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB1000\fR
T}	T{
Recordings this listener keeps,\: removing the oldest it wrote. It bounds what the proxy leaves behind;\: anything that must be kept belongs somewhere the proxy does not prune
T}
T{
\fBcommands\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Record \fBexec\fR sessions too,\: not only the ones with a terminal
T}
.TE
.PP
The header carries the terminal size from \fBpty\-req\fR, the login and the target, and for an \fBexec\fR the command. A \fBwindow\-change\fR becomes a resize event, so a session that was widened replays at both widths rather than wrapping everything after it in the wrong place. Both of the target\(aqs streams are recorded: a terminal does not keep stdout and stderr apart either, and a recording without stderr would be missing exactly the errors.
.PP
An \fBsftp\fR channel is not recorded. It is not a terminal, and its own \fBsftp\fR log line already says what each request did.
.PP
\fBThese files hold everything the session showed.\fR On an administrative session that is a list of everything worth having \[u2014] keys printed, configuration read, tokens echoed. They are written \fB0600\fR by the proxy user, with \fBO_EXCL\fR and proxy\-chosen names, in a directory the operator names; the directory deserves the care the credentials in it will deserve. \fBinput: true\fR goes further still, and is the difference between watching over a shoulder and running a keylogger: it is off by default, it warns when set, and whether it is lawful where you are is not a question this configuration can answer.
.PP
A recording that cannot be opened does not stop the session: it is an error in the log and an \fBssh_recording_failed\fR event, because a bastion that refuses work when a disk fills is its own outage. One that stops part way is logged as short, with the reason.
.SS "server.listeners[].ssh.mfa"
.PP
A second factor after the key or the password. The client is told authentication partially succeeded (RFC 4252 partial success) and is then asked, over keyboard\-interactive, for a one\-time code (RFC 6238 TOTP over the HMAC\-OTP of RFC 4226). Nothing about the session exists until the code verifies: a key alone opens no channel.
.PP
The same section shape, the same enrolment file and the same rules serve the HTTP \fBmfa\fR filter. That is deliberate \[u2014] a second factor that means different things on different ports is not a second factor, because the weakest door decides.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbw(0.9i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lw(0.9i) lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBfile\fR
T}	T{
path
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
The enrolment file (\fBxproxyctl mfa enrol\fR writes the lines). Refused if world readable: it holds every second factor. Re\-read when it changes on disk,\: at most once a second,\: so enrolling and removing take effect without a reload
T}
T{
\fBissuer\fR
T}	T{
name
T}	T{
\fBxproxy\fR
T}	T{
The name an authenticator application shows
T}
T{
\fBprompt\fR
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
\fBOne\-\:time code:\: \fR
T}	T{
What the user is asked
T}
T{
\fBskew\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB1\fR
T}	T{
Steps either side of now that are accepted,\: for a clock that is a little off. Each step is a window an observed code can be replayed in,\: so above 2 it warns
T}
T{
\fBrequire_\:enrolment\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Refuse a user with no enrolment. \fBfalse\fR warns: the account that never enrolled is the one an attacker will use
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:failures\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB5\fR
T}	T{
Failures within \fBwindow\fR before the user is locked out
T}
T{
\fBwindow\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB5m\fR
T}	T{
The window failures are counted in
T}
T{
\fBlockout\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB15m\fR
T}	T{
How long a locked user stays locked
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:users\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB10000\fR
T}	T{
The table that remembers spent codes and recent failures. When it is full the least recently seen entries are dropped,\: which loses replay memory for idle users rather than refusing everyone
T}
.TE
.PP
A code is spent when it is used: a later attempt at the same step, or at an earlier one, is refused even though it verifies. The memory is per process, so in a cluster a code can be replayed once per node \[u2014] put the listener behind one node where that matters, or keep \fBskew\fR at 0.
.PP
What the client is told never distinguishes a wrong code from a replayed one, from a locked account, or from a name that never enrolled. The prompt is shown even to a user with no enrolment, because refusing before asking says the name is not enrolled.
.PP
The file can also be changed while the proxy runs, through the management API (\fB/v1/mfa*\fR), the GUI\(aqs MFA page or the TUI\(aqs MFA screen \[u2014] enrol, replace recovery codes, remove, unlock. An enrolment is shared by every listener reading the same file; a lockout is not, since it lives in the process that counted the wrong codes. See \fBdocs/USAGE.md\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].ssh.sftp"
.PP
Without this section the proxy can say only that a session may use \fBsftp\fR. That is the difference between reading a file and deleting a tree: the whole of it happens inside the channel. With it, each SFTP request is decided, and a refusal is answered with a permission\-denied status, so the session continues and the client is told which operation was refused rather than losing its connection.
.PP
Only version 3 is parsed. A client that negotiates higher is refused at the version exchange, because packets this cannot read are packets whose policy would be guesswork.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.1i) lbw(0.7i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(1.1i) lw(0.7i) lw(0.8i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBread_\:only\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Refuse everything that changes the server: write,\: setstat,\: remove,\: mkdir,\: rmdir,\: rename,\: symlink,\: an open with any writing flag,\: and the extensions whose meaning the proxy does not know
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:paths\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR (any)
T}	T{
Paths a request may name: a glob where \fB*\fR does not cross a slash,\: or a prefix ending in \fB/\fR or \fB/\:**\fR for a whole tree
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:paths\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Refused whatever the allow list says
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:operations\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Operations refused by name: \fBopen\fR,\: \fBread\fR,\: \fBwrite\fR,\: \fBremove\fR,\: \fBrename\fR,\: \fBsymlink\fR,\: \fBsetstat\fR,\: \fBreadlink\fR,\: \fBextended\fR,\: \[u2026]
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:extensions\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR (any)
T}	T{
File extensions a name may carry,\: written without the dot and compared without case. Checked on \fBopen\fR,\: and on both names of a \fBrename\fR or a \fBsymlink\fR \[u2014] the requests that decide what a file is called. A name claiming no extension claims nothing to refuse and passes
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:extensions\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Refused whatever the allow list says. Every extension a name carries is read,\: not only the last,\: so \fBinvoice.\:pdf.\:exe\fR is an exe
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:file_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB0\fR (none)
T}	T{
What one open file may be written. Counted from the highest offset a write reaches,\: not from the bytes sent,\: so writing out of order does not walk past it
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:open_\:files\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB256\fR
T}	T{
Handles one session may have open at once,\: which is what the per\-file state costs
T}
T{
\fByara\fR
T}	T{
object
T}	T{
none
T}	T{
Rules over what is written,\: per file;\: the same section as a \fBtcp\fR listener\(aqs \fByara\fR minus \fBdirections\fR,\: which is not read here. See below
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:packet_\:size\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB262144\fR
T}	T{
One SFTP packet;\: 4096..16777216
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBallow_paths\fR and \fBdeny_paths\fR may carry \fB{user}\fR and \fB{principal}\fR, substituted once per session from the login the client authenticated as and the \fBprincipals\fR entry covering its key. That is how one listener says "your own directory and no other" instead of one list naming everybody\(aqs. A name that could change what a pattern means \[u2014] anything outside letters, digits, \fB\-\fR, \fB_\fR and \fB.\fR, anything over 64 characters, or a name that is only dots \[u2014] refuses the session rather than being substituted or escaped: a login of \fB../..\fR expanded into an allow list is an allow list for somebody else\(aqs directory. \fB{principal}\fR on a listener with no \fBprincipals\fR fails the load, since no session would have a name to put there. An unknown substitution is a load error too, not a literal: \fB{usr}\fR left as it stands matches nothing, which on an allow list refuses everybody and on a deny list refuses nobody.
.PP
\fBWrites get two more checks, because a write is the one request whose content the proxy can see.\fR \fBmax_file_bytes\fR bounds the file the writes make. \fByara\fR runs the rule set over what goes into each file separately: a rule about a file\(aqs first bytes is a rule about a file, and two uploads interleaved on one channel are two files, so each open handle gets its own scanner. A match refuses that write with permission denied and writes a \fByara_match\fR security event naming the path; with the section\(aqs \fBaction: close\fR the transfer ends there rather than only the one packet. Both need to know which handle is which file, so a write on a handle whose \fBopen\fR this proxy never decided on is refused: a write that cannot be held to a bound is not a write to pass on.
.PP
A path that climbs above its own root after cleaning (\fB../../etc/shadow\fR) is refused rather than matched: what it means depends on a working directory the proxy cannot see, and a check on a path whose meaning is unknown is not a check. Absolute paths always work, so nothing legitimate needs the other form.
.PP
##### server.listeners[].ssh.sftp.icap
.PP
Hands written files to a scanning service (RFC 3507) named in \fBicap.services\fR.
.PP
.TS
