.SS "upstreams[].origin_signature"
.PP
Network filtering keeps most traffic off an origin, but not where the origin is reachable from the internet by design (a cloud service, a shared host) or where another tenant sits on the same network. A signed header lets the origin refuse anything that did not pass through the proxy. Every forwarded request carries
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
X\-Xproxy\-Signature: v1;t=<unix seconds>;kid=<key id>;sig=<base64url HMAC\-SHA256>
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
where the MAC covers, as newline separated lines: \fBv1\fR, the method, the \fBHost\fR sent upstream (lower case), the path, the raw query, the timestamp, the client address (the \fBX\-Real\-Ip\fR value), the request id (\fBX\-Request\-Id\fR) and the values of the \fBinclude\fR headers in order (repeated values joined with commas). The key id is the first eight hex digits of the SHA\-256 of the key. A signature header sent by a client is always replaced.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.8i) lbw(0.9i) lbw(1.0i) lbx
lw(0.8i) lw(0.9i) lw(1.0i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBheader\fR
T}	T{
name
T}	T{
\fBX\-\:Xproxy\-\:Signature\fR
T}	T{

T}
T{
\fBsecret_\:file\fR
T}	T{
path
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
Keyring shared with the origin (mode \fB0600\fR,\: created on first start;\: \fBxproxyctl rotate\-\:secret\fR adds a new primary and keeps the previous key,\: so the origin verifies with either while it is updated)
T}
T{
\fBttl\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB5m\fR
T}	T{
Age the origin should accept;\: a signature is also refused more than a minute in the future
T}
T{
\fBinclude\fR
T}	T{
list of headers
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Extra request headers covered,\: for example a tenant header the proxy sets
T}
T{
\fBbody_\:digest\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Cover the request body as well: the proxy buffers it,\: appends \fBsha\-\:256=\::\:<base64>:\fR as a final signed line,\: sends it as \fBContent\-\:Digest\fR and adds \fB;bd=\:1\fR to the signature header. Without it a signature proves that a request passed through the proxy,\: not what it carried \[u2014] anything that can reach the origin can replay a captured header set with a body of its own while the timestamp is inside the TTL. A bodied request larger than 8 MiB is refused with \fB413\fR rather than forwarded with a signature that stops at the headers
T}
.TE
.PP
At the origin, recompute the MAC with the shared key selected by \fBkid\fR, compare in constant time, and refuse when it differs, the key id is unknown or \fBt\fR is older than the TTL. When the header carries \fBbd=1\fR, the last signed line is the body digest: hash the body and compare. Dropping \fBbd=1\fR does not turn that off, because the digest is part of what was signed.
.PP
Within the TTL a signature is replayable by anything that captured it (a log, a proxy in front of the origin, a browser extension). Where that matters, have the origin remember the \fBX\-Request\-Id\fR values it has already answered for the TTL and refuse a repeat: the proxy sets a fresh one per request and the id is covered by the signature, so a replay carries the same id. \fBbody_digest\fR bounds what a replay can change; the id dedupe stops the replay itself. HARDENING.md has verifier snippets; \fBinternal/originsig\fR has \fBVerify\fR for origins written in Go. Combine with mutual TLS (\fBtls.client_cert_file\fR) and with network filtering: each closes what the others cannot.
.SH "routes[]"
.PP
Matching: exact host, then wildcard host, then hostless routes; within a host the longest path (a \fBpath_regex\fR entry counts as the length of its literal prefix and, at equal length, beats a plain prefix); then the number of \fBheaders\fR and \fBcookies\fR conditions (more first, so a conditioned route is tried before the plain route on the same path); then \fBpriority\fR (higher wins); then configuration order. \fBmethods\fR and the conditions are filters: a route whose method set or conditions do not match is skipped and the next candidate is tried.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.4i) lbw(1.1i) lbw(1.4i) lbx
lw(1.4i) lw(1.1i) lw(1.4i) lx.
