.PP
\fBrobots\fR and \fBsitemap\fR are the two to serve honestly: they name the decoy paths, so a crawler that reads either and then requests them has told you what it is. Give those two \fBmark: 0s\fR, so that reading the file marks nobody and only asking for what it names does. \fBexamples/security/honeypots.yaml\fR wires the whole table up, one route per decoy with the paths each is worth serving on; a test fails if a decoy in the table has no route there. \fBxproxyctl honeypot\fR and \fBGET /v1/honeypot\fR both list the names this build carries, which is the authority when a configuration is refused for an unknown decoy.
.PP
\fBresponse_headers\fR apply, so a decoy can carry a \fBServer\fR header of its own. \fBGET /v1/honeypot\fR lists marked clients (address, route, hits, first, last, expires) and the decoy names; \fBDELETE /v1/honeypot?ip=\fR and \fBxproxyctl honeypot forget IP\fR remove a mark. The mark table holds at most 65536 addresses. Counters: \fBhoneypot_hits\fR, \fBhoneypot_marked\fR; metrics \fBxproxy_honeypot_hits_total\fR, \fBxproxy_honeypot_marked\fR.
.SS "routes[].websocket_guard"
.PP
An upgraded connection is the one place a request\-oriented proxy stops looking. Everything before the 101 goes through routing, the WAF, the filters and the logs; everything after it is an opaque byte stream that happens to be travelling over a connection the proxy opened. Applications put their real API in there \[u2014] chat, trading, terminals, GraphQL subscriptions \[u2014] so a proxy that stops at the handshake is guarding the doorway of a building with no walls.
.PP
\fBwebsocket_guard\fR parses RFC 6455 frames in \fBboth\fR directions and applies structure, bounds and patterns. It never rewrites a frame: a violation closes the connection with a close code that says why, or is only recorded, depending on \fBaction\fR.
.PP
.TS
