.PP
The structural checks are the half with no false positives, because they are the protocol\(aqs own rules: a reserved bit set without a negotiated extension, a reserved opcode, an unmasked client frame or a masked server one, a fragmented or oversize control frame, a continuation with nothing to continue, a new message before the previous one finished, a close frame with one byte of status, a close code that must never appear on the wire, a close reason or text message that is not UTF\-8. A client that does any of these is not a browser.
.PP
The bounds are the half worth thinking about: \fBmax_frame_bytes\fR and \fBmax_message_bytes\fR are what stop one connection deciding how much memory the proxy uses, and \fBmessages_per_second\fR is what stops it deciding how much CPU the origin uses. The pattern list is the part with a real false\-positive rate \[u2014] start with \fBaction: log\fR and read \fBxproxy_websocket_violations_total{route}\fR for a week.
.PP
Messages larger than \fBmax_inspect_bytes\fR are checked up to that bound and forwarded: the alternative is buffering whatever a client chooses to send. Compressed frames (\fBpermessage\-deflate\fR) cannot be inspected at all, which is why a reserved bit is refused rather than ignored \[u2014] a negotiated compression extension would silently turn every check off.
.PP
Violations are security events with reason \fBwebsocket\fR, counted per route by \fBxproxyctl\fR and \fBGET /v1/websocket\fR, and exported as \fBxproxy_websocket_violations_total\fR and \fBxproxy_websocket_closed_total\fR.
.SS "routes[].deceive"
.PP
A refusal is information. A scanner that gets 403 has learned that the request it sent was the interesting one, and it will vary that request until something is not refused \[u2014] the refusal is the oracle that tells it when it has found the way through. \fBdeceive\fR answers a client the route no longer trusts with something ordinary instead: the crawl completes, the data is wrong, and the request that would have worked looks exactly like the one that did not.
.PP
The origin is never asked, so a deceived write is discarded. That is the point for a \fBPOST\fR, and it is why the conditions are worth being sure of: a false positive means a real client quietly loses data.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
routes:
  \- name: api
    paths: [/api]
    upstream: app
    deceive:
      marked: true            # a honeypot or honeytoken marked it
      bot_score_at: 80        # or a bot_score filter scored it
      status: 200
      body: '{"items":[],"total":0}'
      content_type: application/json
      mark: 1h                # keep it on the same answer
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
A route must name at least one condition; validation refuses a \fBdeceive\fR block that would admit everyone, and warns on every route that has one, because this is the one control whose failure looks like success.
.PP
.TS
