Field values may not contain a line break or a control character: a newline would end the field and begin another, so a value carrying one could add a \fBContact\fR of somebody else\(aqs choosing to the document this proxy serves. The response is \fBtext/plain; charset=utf\-8\fR with \fBX\-Content\-Type\-Options: nosniff\fR, and only \fBGET\fR and \fBHEAD\fR are answered \[u2014] a \fBsecurity.txt\fR is a file and nothing else.
.PP
The signed form is worth the trouble on a public document: sign it once, put it in \fBbody_file\fR, and a reload picks up a re\-signed file without a restart. \fBvalid_for\fR cannot refresh a signed document, so give a signed one an explicit \fBExpires\fR inside the signature and re\-sign before it lapses; \fBxproxyctl stats\fR reports how many requests each entry answered, which is how you notice a document nobody reads.
.SH "virtual_patches[]"
.PP
A virtual patch blocks a known vulnerability by the shape of the requests that exploit it, while the application is being fixed, and records how often it fired. Patches run right after route matching, before rate limits, filters and the WAF, so they cost nothing for other traffic and need no \fBwaf\fR section. Every listed condition must hold for a patch to apply; at least one of \fBpaths\fR, \fBpath_regex\fR, \fBquery\fR, \fBheaders\fR, \fBcookies\fR or \fBbody\fR is required. A match with \fBaction: block\fR answers \fBstatus\fR with reason \fBvirtual_patch\fR and the id as \fBdetail\fR (ban category \fBvirtual_patch\fR); with \fBaction: log\fR the request continues and the security event and the access log carry \fBvirtual_patch: <id>\fR. \fBxproxyctl patches\fR and \fBGET /v1/patches\fR list the patches with hits, last hit, expiry and state; \fBxproxy_virtual_patch_hits_total{patch}\fR counts per patch and hits survive reloads.
.PP
.TS
