.RE
.PP
The timing check needs no JavaScript and no cookie: the filter remembers when the client address last fetched a page under \fBform_paths\fR and compares. A client with no fetch on record is allowed, because a form page can be cached, prerendered or served by another node; set \fBrequire_fetch\fR only where the deployment makes that impossible. Only \fBapplication/x\-www\-form\-urlencoded\fR bodies are parsed \[u2014] a JSON API sharing the route is none of this filter\(aqs business \[u2014] and the body is replayed byte for byte, so the application receives exactly what the client sent.
.PP
Denies are logged with the configured reason and a detail, and add \fBform_guard\fR (and \fBform_seconds\fR for a timing refusal) to the access log line. A ban trigger names a built\-in reason, so set \fBreason: honeypot\fR to let one pick these denies up; left unset, they are logged under the filter\(aqs own name and ban nobody.
.SS "Kind \fBaccount_guard\fR"
.PP
Protects the endpoints where accounts are attacked. Each endpoint has a class with a default ladder of progressive actions over a window: \fBlogin\fR counts failed attempts (recognised in the response) per client address, per account, per address and account pair, distinct accounts per address (credential stuffing) and distinct addresses per account (spraying, distributed brute force); \fBregister\fR, \fBreset\fR, \fBcart\fR and \fBscrape\fR count requests; \fBcustom\fR needs its own steps. A step fires when any of its thresholds is reached and the highest firing step acts: \fBlog\fR records, \fBdelay\fR holds the request, \fBchallenge\fR serves the browser challenge to unverified clients (a plain 403 without a \fBchallenge\fR section), \fBcaptcha\fR serves the CAPTCHA tier (\fBchallenge.captcha\fR; the proof of work without one) to clients that have not passed it, and \fBblock\fR refuses the key that crossed the threshold for \fBduration\fR, on every node of a cluster. Blocks and denials use reason \fBaccount_abuse\fR (a ban trigger category) with status \fBblock_status\fR; the access log carries \fBaccount_endpoint\fR, \fBaccount_action\fR, \fBaccount_by\fR, \fBaccount_counts\fR, \fBaccount_hash\fR, \fBaccount_campaign\fR, \fBaccount_device\fR and \fBaccount_outcome\fR; \fBxproxyctl accounts\fR and \fBGET /v1/accounts\fR show the live state (tracked keys, active blocks, campaigns) and the process wide counters, which the \fBxproxy_account_*\fR metric families export. Identities are trimmed, lower cased and hashed before they are counted or logged. A campaign spread over many addresses, each under its own thresholds, is detected from the endpoint\(aqs totals (\fBdistributed\fR): while it lasts every unverified request of the endpoint is challenged (or blocked).
.PP
.TS
