.SH "capture"
.PP
Present and \fBenabled: true\fR means the proxy can write the exchanges it handled as pcapng files, which Wireshark, tshark and any other pcap reader open directly.
.PP
The proxy terminates TLS, so there is no point on the wire where the exchange is both complete and readable: in front of the proxy it is ciphertext, and behind it the client is gone and the request carries the proxy\(aqs own address. What this section writes is the proxy\(aqs view \[u2014] the request as it arrived after the header edits, the response as the client received it \[u2014] synthesised into a TCP conversation (handshake, data segments at a 1460 byte MTU, orderly close) between the real client address and the listener address, so a dissector reads it as HTTP and \fBFollow TCP stream\fR shows the exchange. It is a faithful record of what the proxy saw and sent, not a byte for byte record of the packets that carried it: segment boundaries, sequence numbers and the frame timestamps are the proxy\(aqs, and TLS, HTTP/2 framing and HTTP/3 are gone by the time it is written (an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 exchange is rendered with an \fBHTTP/1.1\fR start line, because a dissector needs one).
.PP
> \fBThe files hold decrypted traffic.\fR Session cookies, bearer tokens, > API keys and whatever personal data the application carries are in > them in the clear. They are created \fB0600\fR in a directory the operator > names, the proxy chooses the file names and writes nothing else there, > and \fBredact\fR blanks the header values that should not be on disk at > all \[u2014] but the directory still has to be treated like an access log > with redaction turned off: not on a shared volume, not in a backup > that travels, and removed when the investigation is over. Bodies are > off by default for the same reason.
.PP
Recording has two independent switches, and both have to be on for a byte to be written. The configuration decides \fIwhat may be\fR captured \[u2014] \fBenabled\fR, and the \fBrules\fR that select flows \[u2014] and it reloads. The runtime switch decides \fIwhether it is\fR being captured now, and it is off unless \fBstart_active\fR is set: \fBxproxyctl capture start\fR turns it on, \fBxproxyctl capture stop\fR turns it off, and it turns itself off after \fBmax_duration\fR so a capture started during an incident cannot be left running for a month (a capture that began at start\-up because \fBstart_active\fR is set runs until something turns it off: the bound is on the window an operator opens). That shape is deliberate: the usual deployment carries a capture section that is ready and idle, and an operator throws the switch for one reproduction. A reload keeps the switch exactly as it was, deadline included.
.PP
\fBrules\fR is where "only a certain flow, or all flows" is decided. A rule matches when every selector it names holds; a rule that names no selector matches everything, and a section with rules omitted behaves as one such rule. The first rule that matches decides, so the broad catch\-all goes last. Selectors that are only known once the exchange is over \[u2014] \fBstatuses\fR, \fBreasons\fR, \fBdenied\fR \[u2014] hold the request and response until then and write the whole flow retrospectively, so a rule for "the 403s only" still produces a complete conversation. That includes a refusal decided before routing (a ban, the maintenance gate, a malformed \fBHost\fR): there is no route to match on, so only the answer\-side selectors can want it, and it is written without bodies because nothing read them.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
capture:
  enabled: true
  directory: /var/lib/xproxy/capture
  max_file_bytes: 67108864      # 64 MiB, then rotate
  max_files: 4                  # keep four, oldest removed
  max_duration: 30m             # a window an operator forgets ends itself
  bodies: true
  max_body_bytes: 65536
  redact: [authorization, cookie, set\-cookie, x\-api\-key]
  rules:
    # The reproduction: one client, one API route, everything it does.
    \- name: reported\-client
      routes: [api]
      client_cidrs: ["198.51.100.7/32"]

    # Every refusal, whatever route it was on and whatever refused it.
    \- name: denials
      denied: true
      max_flows: 500

    # The WAF's own decisions, for tuning a rule that is too eager.
    \- name: waf
      reasons: [waf]

    # A thousandth of the upload traffic, as a baseline.
    \- name: sample\-uploads
      methods: [POST, PUT]
      paths: [/upload]
      percent: 1
      max_flows: 200
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBGET /v1/capture\fR and \fBxproxyctl capture status\fR show whether recording is on, when the window ends, the current file and the counters below; \fBPOST /v1/capture\fR with \fB{"active": true, "duration": "10m"}\fR (or \fBxproxyctl capture start \-duration 10m\fR) opens a window and \fB{"active": false}\fR closes it. \fBxproxy_capture_flows_total{result}\fR counts \fBcaptured\fR, \fBskipped\fR, \fBdropped\fR and \fBfailed\fR, \fBxproxy_capture_bytes_total\fR the bytes written, and \fBxproxy_capture_active\fR is 1 while a capture is running \[u2014] worth an alert, since a capture left on keeps writing decrypted traffic to disk. Both mutations are audited with the caller\(aqs uid, gid and pid.
.PP
The web GUI does not offer any of this. It is a network service, and starting a capture writes decrypted traffic to disk; the switch stays on the management socket, where the kernel decides who may throw it.
.PP
.TS
